course: dictionary
lesson: 1421
title: Exaggerated drawings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A caricature is a funny drawing or description that makes some features look much bigger or sillier than they really are. The example says, «Political cartoons make a point about the news with drawings; a caricature stretches someone's features, and leaders are often drawn as animals.» The word stretches is your clue: the features are pulled bigger than real life.
explain: Ask yourself what the drawing is for. A caricature is not meant to be an exact copy. It is meant to make a point, or to make someone look silly. Leaders drawn as animals are not really animals, so the picture is exaggerating to get a message across.
explain: Caricature can be a noun, «a caricature», or a verb, «to caricature». As a verb it means to draw or describe someone in this exaggerated way. Look for the phrases «a caricature of» and «draw a caricature» to spot it in your reading.
fact: A caricature is a funny drawing of a person that makes some features look much bigger than they are, like a big nose or ears.
fact: A caricature can also be a description that makes someone seem silly by exaggerating them.
fact: As a verb, «caricature» means to draw or describe someone in this exaggerated way.
fact: Caricatures are used in art lessons, and in history and media lessons, where political cartoons use them to make a point.
fact: Useful phrases are «a caricature of» and «draw a caricature».
exercise: «The cartoon of the mayor had a huge nose and tiny feet.» What kind of drawing is this? | A map | A photograph | *A caricature | A portrait
exercise: «The artist caricatured the king with enormous ears.» What does caricatured mean here? | Wrote a long poem about him | Measured his height carefully | *Drew him in an exaggerated, silly way | Painted him in soft colours
exercise: «The story described the teacher as a silly cartoon who only shouts.» Why might an author use a caricature like this? | To show the teacher is a real person with real feelings | To make the story shorter | To explain how to paint a picture | *To make the character seem silly by exaggerating them
test: In a political cartoon, why is a caricature useful? | To hide the person's face | *To stretch someone's features so the point is clear | To make the drawing smaller and harder to see | To copy a photograph exactly
test: Which phrase best matches the meaning of a caricature? | An exact photo of someone | *A silly, exaggerated picture of someone | A quiet walk in the park | A long list of facts
exam: Which sentence uses caricature correctly? | *She drew a caricature of her teacher with a tiny head and a giant smile | The weather was a caricature of sunshine today | The bus caricature was waiting at the stop | I caricatured my bag into the lunch box
